Message-ID: <3BLvY1bIrqE.hF61Dh5MUHEJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <3BLvY1bIrqE.BqVfKaLlq1EJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
References: <3BLvY1bIrqE.BqVfKaLlq1EJ@ggplot2.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/ggplot2/c/3BLvY1bIrqE/m/hF61Dh5MUHEJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

You can always specify it in the function itself with a little rigging.

n <- 101
x <- seq(0, 2*pi, length.out = n)
df <- data.frame(
  x = x,
  y = sin(x) + rnorm(n, sd = .2)
)

f <- function(x){
  x[x < 1]   <- NA
  x[x > 5] <- NA
  suppressWarnings(sin(x))
}

qplot(x, y, data = df) +
  stat_function(fun = f, colour = 'red', size = 1)

Otherwise, you could put down another layer with a path, but the above is probably easier.

Cheers
david.

